# Ava — ROCm profile. Ollama with AMD GPU acceleration.
# deploy/install.sh selects this for an AMD box (discrete Radeon or a Strix Halo /
# Ryzen AI Max APU).
#
#   cd deploy && cp profiles/rocm.env .env && docker compose up -d
#
# FIRST RUN pulls the model (install.sh does this for you):
#   docker compose exec ollama-rocm ollama pull llama3.2
#
# Why Ollama and not vLLM. Ollama ships a ROCm build that handles AMD device
# discovery itself, which makes it the portable GPU path — one image, both vendor
# families. vLLM-on-ROCm is real but build- and gfx-target-specific, and steering
# an AMD owner into a tens-of-GB weight pull their build cannot serve is the trap
# `models.engine_servable_here()` exists to prevent. Set
# `inference.allow_vllm_rocm: true` in ava.yaml if you know your ROCm vLLM works.
#
# REQUIREMENTS on the host:
#   * the amdgpu kernel driver loaded, so /dev/kfd and /dev/dri exist
#   * your user in the `render` and `video` groups
# install.sh checks both and falls back to the cpu profile rather than starting a
# service that cannot see the device.
#
# ⚠️ NOT VERIFIED ON DEVICE. This profile is written against the documented ROCm
# container contract and is exercised only by simulation — see the `linux-amd-*`
# rows in deploy/platforms.conf, which read `ci-simulated`. If you run it on real
# AMD hardware, `python3 tools/ondevice_check.py --record` and open an issue with
# the report; that is what promotes the row.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=rocm

AVA_BACKEND_URL=http://ollama-rocm:11434/v1
AVA_BACKEND_ENGINE=ollama
# REQUIRED — Ava ships no default model. Any Ollama tag you have pulled.
AVA_MODEL=

# Optional: pin the COMMIT, not just the repo. Unset, vLLM resolves the model's
# default branch tip on every start, so the weights can change under a box that
# has been serving happily for a month. Use the same value as
# `models.<role>.revision` in ava.yaml, which is what `ava models pull` fetches
# — set one without the other and the pin stops at the download.
AVA_MODEL_REVISION=
